  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-330631",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14259374/assets/597125714057269b7adf9c0a2a9bbdde9631cf37bc80518281ab4444c3189717.png)\n\nFor a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ we have the following symmetry property for $a>0:$\n\n$$\nP(Z>a)=P(Z<-a)=Φ(-a)\n$$\n\nWe're given that $Z∼N(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(Z>0.33).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14259374/assets/98cc48cff914158a4c8facf34de162a237e07ba271067aa51ba719a66f17d8af.png)\n\nBy symmetry, this equals the area corresponding to the probability $P(Z<-0.33).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14259374/assets/357a942bf0b8976c29eedb7f865c381a46c3f841eadfc2203ca3192cea8275af.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Z>0.33)=P(Z<-0.33)=Φ(-0.33)=0.3707.\n$$",
